</div>

<p>(a) Find angle \(ADB\). [[input:ans1]]\(^\circ\) [[validation:ans1]]</p>
<div>[[feedback:prt1]]</div>

<p>(b) Find the length of the diameter \(AB\). [[input:ans2]] cm [[validation:ans2]]</p>
<div>[[feedback:prt2]]</div>

<p>(c) Find the radius of the circle. [[input:ans3]] cm [[validation:ans3]]</p>
<div>[[feedback:prt3]]</div>]]></text>
    </questiontext>
    <generalfeedback format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[<h4>Worked solution</h4>
<p><strong>(a)</strong> \(AB\) is a diameter, so the angle it subtends at any point on the circumference is a right angle. \(D\) is on the circumference, so
\(ADB = 90^\circ\).</p>
<p><strong>(b)</strong> \(C\) is on the circumference too, so \(ACB = 90^\circ\) and triangle \(ABC\) is right-angled at \(C\), with the diameter \(AB\) as its hypotenuse. By Pythagoras:</p>
\[ AB^2 = AC^2 + BC^2 = {@p^2@} + {@q^2@} = {@p^2+q^2@}, \qquad AB = \sqrt{{@p^2+q^2@}} = {@ta2@} \text{ cm} \]
<p><strong>(c)</strong> The radius is half the diameter: \(r = {@ta2@} \div 2 = {@ta3@}\) cm.</p>]]></text>
    </generalfeedback>
